<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Defs>

  <!--
    The one edict the combat tier reads (system 9: AI — combat, docs/spec/simworld-spec.md §10). Its own file
    rather than an addition to Edicts.xml: the loader reads every file under this directory, so a new edict
    needs no edit to a file another lane may be mid-edit on (CLAUDE.md).

    This is where RimWorld's draft went. There, the player selects a colonist and drafts them; here the god
    issues a standing order and every citizen it applies to changes posture for as long as it stands. It
    carries no prioritizedWork at all — unlike every other edict, what it changes is not which work gets done
    first but whether an unarmed citizen goes looking for the enemy or waits to be cornered (see
    SimWorld.AI.CombatPostureUtility for exactly what it does and does not change; an armed citizen and a
    cornered one already fight without it, and no edict overrides a pawn whose violence is disabled).

    No requiredEra: a civilization can always call its people to arms, in any age. No obsoleteEra either —
    nothing about growing up makes "everyone fight" stop being a thing a civilization can demand.

    The mood cost is the steepest of the five shipped edicts, and deliberately: the other four ask people to
    work harder at something, this one asks them to go and be killed.
  -->

  <EdictDef>
    <defName>TakeUpArms</defName>
    <label>take up arms</label>
    <description>Every hand that can hold a weapon meets the enemy, armed or not. Nobody waits to be found.</description>
    <moodThought>UnderTakeUpArms</moodThought>
  </EdictDef>

</Defs>
